AB  - Two frequently used automatic instruments for fine particle (PM10) measurements, Tapered Element Oscillating Micro Balance (TEOM) and β-dust monitor, were compared in a heavily polluted area in the Netherlands. As a reference, two filter samplers were added to the series, a Digitel and an Andersen with PM10 heads. Results indicate that the TEOM measures lower concentrations than the β-dust monitor by about 30-40% depending on the site. The TEOM results were more stable and constant in time on a half-hour basis, while the β-dust monitor was much more variable on a short-term basis. The study concludes that the differences between the samplers for PM10 are too high to arrive at a good assessment of the air quality with one instrum
