AU  - Wheeler, A.J.
T2  - Air and Waste Management Association: Symposium on Air Quality Measurement Methods and Technology 2008
AB  - Chempass PM2.5 PEM's compare very well with TEOM's (r 2 = 0.95) as well as between each other in duplicate analysis (r 2 = 0.93). Significant hot/cold seasonal differences were observed between the PEM/TEOM relationships (summer slope = 1.07 vs winter slope = 1.71). These differences are attributed to evaporation of PM volatiles in the TEOM (Allen et al, 1997). HCI and low flow PEMs compared well (r2=0.71). Harvard coarse impactors compared well with TEOM's in the summer (r 2=0.92) but poorly in the winter (r2=0.07). A regression analysis of OMNI's collocated with TEOM's and HCI's revealed equal intercepts (1.01, and 1.64 respectively) but significantly different slopes between seasons. Further analysis will focus on the effects of meteorological conditions on sampler performance. While the majority of associations between samplers and reference methods are good, there are some bias's that need to be investigated before using the data for exposure research.
