TY  - CONF
TI  - A comparison of PM10 survey monitors
AU  - Lanane, C.
AU  - Johnson, D.
T2  - Air and Waste Management Association - Symposium on Air Quality Measurement Methods and Technology 2010
AB  - The results of the monitor comparison study at T-8 indicate that: 1. Dust Trak and E-Sampler readings taken directly from the instruments could be more than 100 times lower than actual PM10 concentrations. 2. Each sampler had a measurement relationship to actual PM10 measurements that could be used to calibrate the portable samplers. 3. After calibrating the portable samplers to the BGI, the median measurement uncertainty of the portable samplers was about 25% for concentrations above 150 μg/m3. It should be noted that both the DustTraks and the E-Samplers utilize a light-scattering measurement technology that is sensitive to changes in particle size, distribution, composition, and humidity. Both the BGI PQ200 and the TEOM 1400a (AB) utilize a mass-measurement technology that can be sensitive to volatile compounds and humidity. In the area around the Owens Lake, which is at an elevation of 3,600 feet above sea level, the average daily relative humidity during the study period was 25±12% RH. There are no significant sources of volatile compounds in the Owens Valley air basin. As surrogates for EPA equivalent or reference method monitors, the DustTraks and E-Samplers did not fare well in this study where the particulate matter being measured was heterogeneous in terms of particle size, shape, distribution, and composition. This issue is largely attributable to the measurement technology itself rather than to the specific manufacturers' implementation of it. After correction, using a factor derived from comparison with a BGI monitor, measurement uncertainty ranged from 24 to 110% at PM10 concentrations less than 150μg/m3. A more accurate and reliable alternative would be to use a basic filter-based reference or equivalent method PM10 monitor like the BGI PQ200 for PM survey studies.
